  {
   "id": "u3",
   "list": "unsorted",
   "status": "filed",
   "title": "The dosa counter that does not take bookings",
   "subtitle": "Field Notes",
   "note": "",
   "image_url": null,
   "canonical": {
    "article": {
     "byline": "R. Okafor",
     "excerpt": "There is no sign outside. You find it by the queue.",
     "hero": null,
     "readingMinutes": 6,
     "siteName": "Field Notes",
     "summary": "A twelve-seat counter on Holly Grove serves one thing well. Go before seven. Order the ghee roast and the filter coffee. We landed in Lisbon the week after.",
     "text": "There is no sign outside. You find it by the queue, which starts at half past five and is gone by seven, because by seven the batter is gone too.\n\nInside are twelve stools, one flat-top and a man who has made the same dosa for nineteen years. He does not hurry and he does not talk while he pours.\n\nThe ghee roast comes first. It is as long as your forearm and it breaks like glass.\n\nThen the sambar, which is thinner than you expect and better for it. Then the coffee, poured from a height into a steel tumbler, and then somebody is standing behind you waiting for the stool.\n\nNobody has written the recipe down. He says the batter knows what day it is, and that is all he will say about it."
    }
   },
   "confidence": 0.9,
   "enriched": false,
